Root cause:
In the `eb_lookup_vmas()` function, when `eb_add_vma()` fails, the code did not set `eb->vma[i].vma` to NULL. The cleanup function `eb_release_vmas()` relies on finding a NULL vma pointer to stop iterating. Since the `eb.vma` array was only partially initialized (only `eb.vma[0].vma` was set to NULL), the remaining elements contained slab poison values. This caused a NULL-pointer dereference when `eb_release_vmas()` attempted to process an invalid vma pointer after a failure in `eb_add_vma()`.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`eb.vma[0].vma = NULL;`
`if (err) return err;` (after `eb_add_vma` failure)
Reason:
The initialization only sets the first element of `eb->vma` to NULL, leaving subsequent elements uninitialized (containing slab poison). When `eb_add_vma` fails, the function returns immediately without setting `eb->vma[i].vma` to NULL. The cleanup function `eb_release_vmas` expects a NULL vma pointer to stop iterating, so it reads a poisoned pointer and causes a NULL-pointer dereference (or invalid memory access).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`memset(eb.vma, 0, (args->buffer_count + 1) * sizeof(struct eb_vma));`
Removal of the `err` label and associated manual NULL assignments in `eb_lookup_vmas`.
Reason:
`memset` zeros the entire array, guaranteeing that every `vma` pointer is NULL. After this change, any early return from `eb_lookup_vmas` leaves the rest of the array properly NULL-initialized, so `eb_release_vmas` will correctly stop at the first NULL pointer. The manual NULL assignments are no longer needed, simplifying the error handling and removing the source of the bug.


